---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Update &amp; rollback — sum">
  <div class="doc">
    <h1>Update &amp; rollback</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Quiescent updates, rollback and runtime releases. An update activates only
      a revision merged on the sum origin default branch, resolved to an
      immutable SHA.
    </p>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2>Update and roll back</h2>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div>./bin/sumctl update check --claim TOKEN          <span class="c"># fetch origin, resolve the merged revision, report default/active/compatibility</span></div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl update apply --claim TOKEN          <span class="c"># stage the release, validate quiescence, switch .local/current, fast-forward a clean clone</span></div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl update status</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl update rollback --claim TOKEN       <span class="c"># reselect the recorded previous approved runtime; records, questions, reports, worktrees stay</span></div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl update recover --claim TOKEN --generation GENERATION</div>
      </div>
      <p>Only the user authorizes update or rollback. An interrupted activation requires its exact journal generation. No core, agent or service is restarted, and dirty or diverged work stays intact. Normal Open/init/status never migrate schema-one state.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2>Next-start adoption</h2>
      <p>Running, starting or uncertain attempts and unresolved launch/delivery effects block activation. Dirty or divergent checkout alignment refuses before runtime selection. Open questions and unapplied answers alone do not block.</p>
      <p>After success, checkout HEAD, selected runtime and entry instructions agree. Start your usual agent here again to read them. Workers keep sealed procedures; approved worker brief revisions retain their existing request/adopt flow.</p>
      <p>Logical ownership and the root state-format fence require a compatible rollback target. Historical task and procedure bytes stay unchanged.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2>Runtime releases</h2>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div>./bin/sumctl release stage            <span class="c"># builds .local/releases/&lt;sha&gt; for HEAD; nothing is activated</span></div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl release list</div>
      </div>
      <p>A release holds the committed Sum tree, native helper, exact published HC 0.1.14, matched manual/template and checksummed manifest. Rollback checks actual target binary capabilities. Legacy schema-one installations remain preserved; live migration is unavailable. Start independently in a fresh clone and use sumctl history for read-only records. An old runtime cannot reactivate an inactive archive. Compatible HC-to-HC updates remain separate from shared-core upgrades.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
